WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.430
Film Courage: Just a few days ago Christopher
you had a piece published on No Film School

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:08.800
which was “10 Production Saving Tips for
Filming in a Remote Location.”

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.510
It was excellent advice and I was just wondering
if we can talk about a few of them?

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.800
Maybe some of which you consider the most
important?

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:22.440
Christopher Smith: I think the most important
thing (hands down) is just the need to do

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:28.830
your homework and prepare before your trip
because information that you get from that

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:34.019
process informs all of the decisions you are
going to have to make throughout the whole

00:00:34.019 --> 00:00:38.399
production in terms of what’s the climate
like?

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:40.510
What’s your resources there?

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.470
Where are the emergency facilities?

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.399
What’s the culture like all?

00:00:45.399 --> 00:00:51.859
Of those things, they inform everything from
what kind of equipment you bring to what kind

00:00:51.859 --> 00:00:54.909
of clothing you bring (like all that stuff).

00:00:54.909 --> 00:01:03.510
But also logistics in terms of transportation
and I think it just really informs it all,

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:04.530
you know?

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:13.740
I think another one might be just the safety
issues in terms of preparing with getting

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:19.659
the right insurance in place just in case
of unforeseen circumstances.

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:25.270
And what you need to take with you in terms
of your well-being and what information you

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:29.399
need in terms of being evacuated or something
like that should something go wrong.

00:01:29.399 --> 00:01:33.409
So yeah, I think those are the two obviously
big ones.

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:40.370
And then there’s lots of follow on pieces
of advice just about gear and what to consider

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:41.370
with that.

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:45.549
Film Courage: I thought what was interesting
was that you said don’t have your gear in

00:01:45.549 --> 00:01:47.570
bags that scream “Hey I’m a filmmaker!

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:48.700
And I have expensive gear!”

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:53.579
Maybe can you talk about some ways to downplay
the fact that you are essentially wearing

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:55.280
thousands of dollars worth of gear on you?

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:03.280
Christopher Smith: Right…That’s a really
tricky one because at times you just can’t

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:10.070
hide it but definitely I opt to not take Pelican
cases or any sort of hard case.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:19.050
And for carry ons, well I carry on my camera
always and I’ll just have like a soft backpack

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.050
camera bag.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:23.900
And I think that’s actually way more practical
than a Pelican case anyways.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.260
But it also tends to look like just a big
backpack (which is nice).

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:35.370
And for checked stuff I opt for really large,
rolling luggage like you would normally see

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:43.280
with a traveler and I just get one that is
long enough to fit my tripod and I make it

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:51.040
all fit into those basically and will pack
in clothes around it to help pad it and then

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:58.370
I’ll use an approved lock to it up which
when you get to developing countries and I

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:06.060
don’t really know how risky it is but I’ve
heard of people who work at the airlines stealing

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:12.320
things out of bags or not even them, just
like hotels or anywhere else it’s a thing

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.510
that can happen so I have a little bit of
security with the lock so at the very least

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:18.630
gives you piece of mind.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.160
Film Courage: That’s great advice.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:26.650
And also to when you mentioned, it’s 2017
and getting through the checkpoint is probably

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:31.780
even more stringent than ever before, what
are some tips on protecting what’s going

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:34.820
through and what if your bag is opened up?

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.340
My bag has been opened up and I didn’t even
have gear in it.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:42.070
Christopher Smith: I mean if you have gear,
they are always going to open your bag.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:48.590
Particularly I’ve noticed tripod bags have
been opened every single time this.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:53.160
And I traveled a lot with my gear over the
years, not just for this project but for another

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.520
film I am working on and also back in the
Tiny days [his movie with co-filmmaker Merete

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:57.930
Mueller TINY: A Story About Living Small]
I was sort of splitting my time between New

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:58.930
York and Colorado.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:02.610
And there was a time where I brought literally
everything I used to make movies with me everywhere

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:09.040
I went including my computer and hard drives
(to edit on), so I have a fair amount of experience

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.040
doing that.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.020
And my experience is, they always check your
bags.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:20.400
The only thing I’ve ever really run into
was I’ve had some Allen keys and I guess

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:27.630
the largest size of that Allen key set wasn’t
approved to be carried on so they confiscated

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:30.930
them.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:35.560
Your carry-on is the think you have to worry
about so having these sort of sharp objects

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:41.210
or tools, put those in your checked luggage
and you won’t risk having the confiscated.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:44.180
Film Courage: What about [what he said previously]
“Look like you belong.”

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:48.010
I know that probably varies and I’m not
even sure if I’m paraphrasing for one of

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:51.672
the tips you mentioned, but you know, fitting
into the culture of a place.

00:04:51.672 --> 00:04:54.900
Like if you are somewhere in Venice Beach
(California) you want to look the part like

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.740
you’re going to the beach not in full regalia.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:06.090
Christopher Smith: I mean it’s sort of impossible
particularly in Cambodia to really look like

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:11.260
you belong but looking like you’re not fresh
off the boat, you know?

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:15.400
You are aware of your surroundings at all
times.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:22.140
You basically want to make yourself look like
not an easy target, rather than a tourist

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.740
who doesn’t sort of know any better.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:29.600
But it looks like you are knowledgable about
how things work in the country and you’re

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:34.280
aware and that’s when I think people are
aware and that’s when people are going to

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:38.290
say “Oh that guy’s a little too difficult”
Or that girl and instead go after the tourist

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.389
(not that they should go after them, we don’t
want that).

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:45.010
Film Courage: Right…well you can always
tell even in Los Angeles who is a tourist

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:49.070
and who is not by how they are kind of looking
around in awe like we’re animals in a zoo

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:50.280
sort of thing.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:55.480
You always talked about riding in taxis and
where to put your bags.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:00.070
Christopher Smith: Definitely.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:03.440
Everything you do in foreign countries is
going to be more expensive because you have

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:10.040
considerations which you don’t when you’re
just traveling which is way more luggage and

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.160
expensive gear.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:17.220
So I usually end up taking single taxis which
in Cambodia is pretty uncommon and reserved

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.620
for people who have a lot of money.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:26.140
Normally they share taxis or tuk-tuks or they
ride on the backs of motorcycles and I’ve

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:28.980
done all of those things in Cambodia.

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:34.310
What I normally do when I arrive with all
of my gear in bags, I take a taxi to the hotel

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:40.050
where I drop it off and if I don’t need
it then I’ll tuk-tuk or ride a motorcycle

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.340
around.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:46.981
But when I’m going out on a shoot I bring
what I need for that shoot and because this

00:06:46.981 --> 00:06:53.520
is a verite shoot oftentimes I don’t always
bring my tripod with me because I do a lot

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:54.520
of mostly handheld stuff.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:59.810
So in that case I can just bring my backpack
and get everything there that I need for the

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:00.810
day.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:04.570
And a lot of my additional gear is more for
interviews and things like that, like lighting

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:13.570
or cables or hard drives that I can offload
later (that I don’t really need for the

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:14.570
shoot).

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:18.550
And then when I ride in tuk-tuks with my gear
or bags, I make sure to keep them in the middle

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:22.850
of the tuk-tuk because people will ride up
next to them and they will have one person

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:26.430
on the back of the motor cycle reach over
and grab your bag and then ride away and your

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:29.650
tuk-tuk will not catch a motorcycle.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:33.941
So you just kind of want to be aware that
this is a possibility, keep your hand on your

00:07:33.941 --> 00:07:38.840
bag and just keep your eye out for suspicious
motorcycles who might be trying to take your

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:39.840
bags.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.350
Film Courage: Well last but not least what
about making polite conversation and someone

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:45.790
asks “Hey, what are you doing here?

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:47.000
What are you up to?”

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:50.211
Do you disclose “I’m a documentary filmmaker”
or do you just say “I’m traveling and

00:07:50.211 --> 00:07:53.600
want to try out my new camera.”

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.930
Christopher Smith: I think that really depends
on who is asking.

00:07:56.930 --> 00:08:04.560
Little kids particularly in these developing
countries just like love not only Westerners

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.070
but when you have a camera and you know about
TV and cameras and what that means and they

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:13.980
get really excited and they want to come over
and see what you’re doing.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:20.770
And I love to show it to them and let them
play with it sometimes if I have time.

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:26.270
If it’s somebody who looks like they are
in a place of authority I do tend to downplay

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:30.440
it a little bit because you don’t want to
call too much attention to yourself.

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:35.969
You never know who is legitimate, who is not.

00:08:35.969 --> 00:08:41.339
Because you can’t help but not know those
things in a country you are not familiar with.

00:08:41.339 --> 00:08:45.180
Just like you wouldn’t do that here if you
didn’t know why somebody was asking you

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.760
questions in a part of town you are not normally
in.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.230
You would probably kind of keep it close to
your chest.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:56.869
But I just say like “Oh, I’m filming…”

00:08:56.869 --> 00:09:04.029
Actually, I don’t actually say what the
project is, I say what I’m filming.

00:09:04.029 --> 00:09:05.079
So if someone says “What are you doing?”

00:09:05.079 --> 00:09:06.259
I say “Oh, I’m filming this fisherman.”

00:09:06.259 --> 00:09:12.430
Or whatever because you are answering the
question but it is not giving away too much.

